WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.740
To celebrate the release of Furious 7, let’s
take a look at 7 things you probably didn’t

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.639
know about the seventh movie in the nitrous-oxide-charged
franchise.

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:16.219
The sequence in the movie that shows Dom and
the Fast family drive out of a cargo plane

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:19.410
in mid-flight was a real practical stunt!

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:24.910
The scene was filmed over three days in the
Arizona desert with a C-130 plane and two

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.870
sets of five driverless cars, each of which
was dropped two or three times from the aircraft

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.860
at heights of 10,000 to 12,000 feet.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:40.350
Camera-mounted helicopters and skydiving camera
operators were used to capture the action.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.180
One of car co-ordinator Dennis McCarthy’s
favourite vehicles in the movie is the off-road

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.530
version of Dom’s 1968 Dodge Charger, which
McCarthy made from scratch with his crew.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.440
It was also the most expensive car to build
for Furious 7.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.471
The real challenge with building that car
was fitting all the custom components of an

00:00:58.471 --> 00:01:04.300
off-road vehicle into the body of the Charger
while still leaving room for Vin Diesel!

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.310
When Dennis McCarthy wanted a particularly
unique car for Dom, he went looking in Las

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.470
Vegas at the annual SEMA car show.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:17.450
That’s where he found a 1968 Dodge Charger
called “Maximus”, which was a custom-fabricated

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:21.159
build by Nelson Racing Engines and Scott Spock
Racing.

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:27.900
The one-off, brushed metal ultra-car has a
2000-horsepower engine and is valued at over

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.150
$1 million.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:35.430
Furious 7’s Abu Dhabi sequence features
W Motors’ Lykan Hypersport, one of the world’s

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.729
fastest and most expensive cars at around
$3.4 million.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.920
The car features white gold, sapphires and
diamonds, and was the most expensive car featured

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.310
in Furious 7.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.280
To keep costs down though, W Motors built
five replicas of the car for the movie, using

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.170
the same molds as their original vehicle but
building the car out of fibreglass instead

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.350
of carbon fibre.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:02.479
Those five replicas were used for filming
the movie’s stunts, while an original Lykan

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:06.409
was used for close-up beauty shots.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:11.920
The luxurious penthouse apartment in Abu Dhabi’s
Etihad Towers where the Fast family crash

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.420
a billionaire’s party was actually a set
built north of Atlanta!

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.700
Filming took place over the course of a week
and the set was made to withstand stunt drivers

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.110
speeding and drifting through it while other
stunt performers, dressed as wealthy party-goers,

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.500
leapt out of the way!

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.020
Several other sets including Hobbs’ office
and the cement factory were also built in

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.890
the same location.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:40.390
The Race Wars scene in Furious 7 was filmed
in Lancaster about 70 miles north of downtown

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:41.390
LA.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:47.030
Letty’s Race Wars ride is a 1970 Plymouth
Barracuda, which we also saw in Fast &amp; Furious

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.030
6.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.650
There were hundreds of extras on location,
including car club members and fans of the

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.670
franchise, and there were more than 300 cars.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.730
Temperatures during filming were over 100
degrees Fahrenheit in the shade.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:05.810
Furious 7 screenwriter Chris Morgan used a
system of colour-coded Post-It notes to keep

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:11.590
track of everything in the Fast &amp; Furious
world, including characters, cars, plot points,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.120
and even Dom’s silver cross pendant!

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.200
Furious 7 is the fifth Fast &amp; Furious movie
written by Morgan.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.480
The first Fast film he wrote was Tokyo Drift.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.700
Well there you have it, 7 things you probably
didn't know about Furious 7!

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.610
